{"id":"h050","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. The change in the Gibbs energy of a certain constant-pressure process was found to fit the expression $\\Delta G / \\text{J}=-85.40+36.5(T / \\text{K})$. Calculate the value of $\\Delta S$ for the process.\nIs the correct answer \"-36.5$\\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1}$\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"At constant pressure, (∂G/∂T)_p = -S, so ΔS = -(∂ΔG/∂T)_p.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The expression is linear in T, so its slope equals the temperature derivative of ΔG, 36.5 J/K.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Therefore ΔS = -36.5 J K^-1.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The form ΔG = ΔH - TΔS matches the expression, giving ΔH = -85.40 J.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The coefficient of T equals -ΔS, so ΔS = -36.5 J K^-1.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"A sign slip is the only possible error, since the slope is positive while ΔS is negative.","confidence":0.3,"lean":-1,"theta":0.4712,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The sign slip concern is resolved by the identity, since the coefficient of T is -ΔS.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The perfect gas and 298.15 K assumptions do not affect the result.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The correct answer is yes, -36.5 J K^-1.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,